  "code_simplified": [
    "Remove the unconditional format Vec clone and its lifetime; retain the original packed header on the stack and restore it exactly on rejected shared-rate overrides. Replace duplicated mode dispatch with one match.",
    "ASCII IDs bypass UTF-8 character decoding: extend contiguous UTF-16 storage directly from u8 values using safe Rust. Unicode continues through encode_utf16.",
    "Remove two unsafe blocks and guid_eq from sample-format recognition. Read bounded little-endian header/GUID bytes, reject unsupported bit widths before reading the GUID, and compare only the subtype required by the bit width."
  ],
  "retention_basis": [
    "Preparation retains a deterministic allocation/copy benefit: normal paths drop from two Rust allocations/80 bytes to one/40; rejected overrides from three/154 bytes to two/114. CPU-time improvement is unproven amid native COM variation.",
    "ASCII encoding improves CPU throughput 1.726x while preserving one allocation and zero reallocations. Unicode changes by about 0.2%, within measurement variation.",
    "Safe recognition improves extensible float32 throughput 1.272x and unsupported format rejection 1.8-2.4x, while eliminating raw pointer casts and an unaligned read."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "The safe parser pays about 0.34-0.41 scheduled CPU cycles for bounds checks on plain formats; extensible PCM16 rises about 0.22 cycles. These small costs are retained for bounds safety and faster extensible float/rejection paths.",
    "Original behavior is preserved for complete format descriptors: accepted tags, bit widths, complete GUID comparisons, ignored metadata, trailing bytes, and returned errors. Truncated descriptors now safely return None; the previous private implementation assumed valid OS buffers.",
    "Kernel microbenchmarks do not measure full application startup or steady-state rendering speed. Preparation CPU samples are dominated by native COM work and show no reliable speedup.",
    "Native COM allocations remain and are excluded from Rust allocation counts.",
    "The prior reservation tradeoff still applies: Unicode IDs can reserve more peak bytes because capacity uses the UTF-8 byte bound.",
    "Windows 7 was compile-checked and tested via legacy COM mocks, not run on Windows 7 hardware. Nightly emits an unrelated unused windows-core workspace dependency warning in its standard-library build."
  ],
